    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d483">

                <pb facs="0529" n="421" sameAs="frus:frus1927v02/pg421"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.512/690: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Chargé in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Mayer</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1927-10-11T13:00:00-05:00"><hi rendition="#italic">October 11,
                                1927—1 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>358. Your 914, October 7, 9 p.m. Your telegram to Canton approved.</p>
                <p>Is the two and one-half percent surtax referred to the so-called consumption and
                    production tax imposed October 11, 1926, mentioned in Canton’s despatch to the
                    Legation of October 8, 1926,<anchor n="65" xml:id="d483-f1"/> and when was it
                    abolished?</p>
                <p>Is Department correct in understanding that new tax is ten percent of two and
                    one-half percent, namely, one-fourth of one percent?</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Kellogg</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
